22:03:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It is completely normal to feel a bit anxious as your son approaches such a big milestone! Starting school is a major transition for both children and parents. To answer your question directly: **You don't need to be overly worried, but it is helpful to be prepared.** Here is some perspective on what to expect and how you can help him succeed: **1. Reframe "Immaturity" as "Development"** While there is a common stereotype that young boys are "immature," the reality is that *all* young children—regardless of gender—are still developing their executive functioning skills. At this age, the parts of the brain responsible for impulse control, sitting still, and emotional regulation are still very much under construction. What looks like "immaturity" is often just a child acting exactly their age. **2. Teachers Expect This** Early childhood educators do not expect children to arrive on the first day with perfect behavior. They expect wiggles, short attention spans, and occasional boundary-testing. A huge part of kindergarten or early elementary school is actually teaching children *how* to do school—how to line up, how to raise a hand, how to share, and how to manage big feelings. Teachers build movement, play, and brain breaks into the day specifically to accommodate young, active bodies and minds. **3. How You Can Prepare Him** Instead of worrying, you can use this time before school starts to help him practice the skills he will need: * **Practice independence:** Have him practice putting on his own shoes, zipping his coat, opening his lunchbox, and using the bathroom independently. * **Practice following directions:** Give him fun, multi-step instructions at home (e.g., "Please grab your blue toy car and put it in the toy box, then come sit with me"). * **Read books about school:** Books can help him visualize what a classroom looks like and what will be expected of him. * **Practice taking turns:** Play simple board games that require him to wait his turn and handle losing gracefully. * **Establish routines:** Start getting him into a consistent bedtime and morning routine a few weeks before school starts so he is well-rested. **4. Partner with the Teacher** Think of you and his teacher as a team. If your son does struggle with certain behaviors in class, a good teacher will reach out to you so you can work on it together. You can also proactively ask the teacher at the first parent-teacher conference, "How is he doing with his listening skills and interacting with peers?" **When to actually worry:** You only need to be concerned if his behavior is extreme—such as being highly aggressive toward other children, experiencing severe anxiety that prevents him from participating, or if he is entirely unable to follow directions after a few months of adjusting. If that happens, the school will work with you to provide the right support. For now, try to focus on the excitement of this new chapter. He is going to learn, grow, and mature so much over this next year!
