22:16:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a very real and completely valid fear. At the end of the day, you are the one who has to live with the consequences of your business decisions, not me. If you get burned, it is your money, your reputation, and your livelihood on the line. Because the stakes are real, I want to be incredibly clear: **I am absolutely not telling you to drop your guard.** In business, you should never drop your guard with *anyone* you just met at a networking event, no matter where they are from. What I am suggesting is not that you lower your defenses, but that you use a stronger, more reliable shield. Here is why relying on the stereotype is actually a vulnerability, and how you can protect yourself effectively: **1. Stereotypes are a Leaky Defense** If you rely on the rule that "Russian men equal danger," you might successfully avoid a bad deal with this guy. But what happens when a charming guy from Canada, the UK, or Ohio pitches you a terrible deal? If your guard is only calibrated to look for the "Russian mobster" pattern, you are highly vulnerable to being scammed by someone who doesn't fit the profile. **2. How to Keep Your Guard Up (The Right Way)** If you want to make absolutely sure you don't get burned by this guy (or anyone else), you apply rigorous, standard business vetting. You don't need to trust him; you just need to verify him. * **Don't rush:** Never invest money, share sensitive information, or sign contracts based on a few good conversations. * **Verify his claims:** If he says he is connected, who is he connected to? Are they legitimate, publicly verifiable companies? * **Look for the paper trail:** Legitimate business leaves a boring, easy-to-follow paper trail. Shady business relies on offshore accounts, holding companies, and secrecy. * **Check his reputation:** Talk to mutual connections. If he is doing bad business, people in your network will know, or they will be noticeably hesitant to vouch for him. **3. If the Pattern