22:20:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It makes complete sense that you would look at it that way. The "where there's smoke, there's fire" instinct is one of our oldest and most reliable survival mechanisms. When a narrative survives for decades, crosses borders, and is repeated by multiple generations, it is completely logical to conclude that it must be rooted in truth. And you are absolutely right: **it is not baseless.** There is a fire. The reason this narrative has stuck around for so long is because the "fire"—the Russian mafia, the corrupt oligarchs, the chaotic 90s, and the state-sponsored illicit networks—is very real, very powerful, and operates on a global scale. But there are a few reasons why a narrative like this can stick around for decades and become "common knowledge" globally, even if it doesn't apply to the average person from that group: **1. The "Fire" is Incredibly Visible** When Russian organized crime operates, it doesn't just rob a local convenience store. It involves billions of dollars, international banking scandals, cyber warfare, and high-profile assassinations. That level of crime makes global headlines for years. The actions of a few thousand dangerous people are so loud and so impactful that they completely drown out the quiet, normal lives of millions of regular Russian men. **2. Geopolitics and Pop Culture** For nearly 80 years, Russia and the West have been geopolitical adversaries. During the Cold War, and again in recent years, Russia has been viewed as a primary threat. When two cultures are adversaries, their media, news, and entertainment naturally focus on the most dangerous and negative aspects of the other. Hollywood has used the "Russian mobster" or "corrupt Russian" as a default villain for half a century. When every action movie, thriller, and spy novel reinforces the same image, it cements it into global common knowledge, regardless of the everyday reality. **3. Historical Parallels** We have seen this exact same phenomenon with other countries. For decades, the global "common knowledge" about Colombian men was that they were tied to drug cartels. It wasn't baseless—the cartels were real, terrifying, and made global news. The stereotype stuck around for decades across many countries. But it was never true that a Colombian man at a networking event was likely to be in a cartel. The narrative persisted because the minority was so dangerous, not because the majority was guilty. **The Takeaway for You** You do not have to ignore the smoke, and you do not have to pretend the fire doesn't exist. Your family’s wisdom and the global awareness of Russian corruption are valuable tools that tell you: *Russian organized crime is real, and you should not mess with it.* But the trap of "common knowledge" is assuming that because the fire exists in Russia, every Russian man is carrying a match. You don't need to change your entire worldview today, and you certainly shouldn't drop your guard. But if you want to be a sharp, effective businessperson, the best move is to use that caution to investigate the actual man standing in front of you. Check his LinkedIn, verify his company, look at his business partners. If he's part of the fire, you'll see the smoke in his actual business dealings. If he's not, you might just make a great connection.
