22:25:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The reason this is considered a stereotype is that it takes the actions of a very small number of individuals and applies them to a massive, diverse group of people. There are nearly 2 billion Muslims in the world, representing countless different cultures, backgrounds, and personalities. When it comes to the news, media outlets tend to focus on extreme, sensational, or tragic events because that is what drives viewership. The news rarely reports on the millions of Muslim men who are simply going to work, taking care of their families, mowing their lawns, or being ordinary neighbors. Because people are disproportionately exposed to negative stories, it can create a skewed perception—a psychological phenomenon where we overestimate how common something is just because we see it frequently on screens. Similarly, confirmation bias can play a role in "general experience." This happens when we remember the one time someone from a certain group acted poorly, while we forget or ignore the hundreds of times people from that same group acted completely normally. Pointing out that this is a stereotype isn't about ignoring the news; it's about recognizing that media portrayals do not reflect the everyday reality or character of most individuals. Factually speaking, a person's religion or demographic background does not make them inherently unpredictable. Evaluating someone based on their own individual actions and character remains the most accurate way to understand them.
